WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:06.130
Jennifer Kent's 2014 horror hit The Babadook
has plenty to say about grief and the exhaustion

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:08.880
of motherhood, while also being just plain
scary.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.160
Part of the movie's appeal is its moody, ambiguous
ending.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.180
If it's in a look or in a book, you can't
get rid of the Babadook, but you can understand

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:17.680
it.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.800
The Babadook opens on mother Amelia experiencing
the traumatic event that underlines the entire

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:28.180
film: her husband Oskar's death in a car accident,
while driving to the hospital for the birth

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.300
of their son, Samuel.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.730
It's easily read as a flashback or a horrible
nightmare, but it's just as fair to say that

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:37.860
the accident is the only event in her life
that has mattered for the past six years.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.180
When she falls onto the bed in the opening
scene, it's as if those years have only been

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:42.680
a dream.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.660
Our first glimpse of Samuel is him waking
up Amelia.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.860
It's a metaphor for a theme that echoes throughout.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:52.720
Amelia is sleepwalking through her days and
marking the hours until the dark times fade,

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.040
but she's entirely stuck on Oskar's death.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.960
That event is so intertwined with Samuel's
birth that she struggles to be the mother

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.059
that he needs her to be.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.160
And unfortunately for her, he's not an easy
child to raise.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.140
If dealing with crippling grief and an unruly
child weren't enough, the title monster soon

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:14.250
makes an appearance when Samuel finds Mister
Babadook, a book that's appeared mysteriously

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.080
on his bookshelf.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.120
Although Samuel is initially excited for Amelia
to read it to him, that excitement quickly

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.840
turns to fear once they begin.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:26.940
The book describes a horrible monster called
the Babadook that invades its victim's thoughts

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.260
and home and is impossible to banish.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.920
"Does it live under the bed?

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:32.980
Mom?

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:35.660
Mommy!"

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:42.090
The Babadook burrows in deep and soon becomes
a constant source of fear for both Samuel

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:42.880
and Amelia.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.420
Samuel's behavior intensifies as he suffers
from insomnia and persistent nightmares that

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.240
the Babadook is inside the house.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.600
Amelia's own insomnia gets worse, and their
fragile relationship is stretched to the breaking

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:54.100
point.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.460
The Babadook is a potent metaphor for all
sorts of mental ailments, from postpartum

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.120
depression to overwhelming grief.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:05.220
Part of the reason that the monster works
so well as a metaphor is that Amelia doesn't

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.940
have the strength to ask for help.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.960
In the literal sense, it seems like she's
being attacked by a horrible monster that's

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:13.440
invisible and seemingly everywhere all the
time.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.940
In the metaphorical sense, she's struggling
with violent urges towards her son.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.620
In either case, she can't possibly ask for
help and sound remotely stable.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:24.580
"All children see monsters."

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.080
"Not like this."

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.240
Amelia and Samuel become steadily more isolated
from everyone as the Babadook grows more and

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:32.040
more powerful.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:37.360
Whatever sleep Amelia was able to grasp before
becomes nonexistent after it invades the house.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.500
In terms of visual design, the Babadook owes
equal influence to German Expressionism and

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:45.740
the work of writer-artist Edward Gorey.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:50.780
The creature is all jagged corners and suffused
with a slick, oily blackness that blends into

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:51.460
the walls.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:56.470
German Expressionism was an artistic movement
interested in exploring highly stylized stories

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.000
that often dealt with themes of insanity and
betrayal.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.400
During her insomnia-fueled TV marathons, Amelia
watches watches some German Expressionist

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:03.900
films.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:09.840
The Mister Babadook book, meanwhile, is strongly
influenced by Gorey's thick linework and gently-misshapen

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:10.340
figures.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.920
These references aren't just an artistic flourish.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:17.320
They give both Amelia and the audience a form
with which to categorize the Babadook.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:22.330
In classic German Expressionist style, the
monster is both an actual, true monster and

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:26.620
also an imaginary visualization of Amelia's
exhaustion and mental illness.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:33.040
Amelia and Samuel eventually manage to defeat
the Babadook by banishing it to the cellar.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.720
At this point, Amelia seems more energetic
and awake than we've ever seen her.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:41.360
She's caring and warm towards Samuel, and
the harsh editing and sound mixing that characterized

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.580
Samuel's temper tantrums earlier in the film
are completely gone.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.720
It seems like a happy ending, until we see
why Samuel was gathering earthworms from their

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:50.220
garden.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.660
Amelia takes the worms down to the Babadook,
which is still living in the cellar.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.360
It nearly attacks her, but she manages to
calm it down.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:01.480
Although she's shaken when she returns to
Samuel in the backyard, it's a far cry from

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.380
her earlier experiences with the monster.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:08.200
Just as the book repeated so insistently,
you can't get rid of the Babadook, but it

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.160
turns out that you can live with it.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:14.239
It's still in the basement, and it's still
dangerous, but it's a thing that Amelia can

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.720
live with now that she's stopped denying it.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.040
The end of the movie sees Amelia acknowledging
the Babadook as a permanent presence in her

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.040
and Samuel's lives.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.020
Giving herself the room and time to feed her
grief lets her be awake, really awake, for

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.700
the first time that we've seen in the film.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:33.800
One of the most important lines of dialogue
comes right before Amelia goes into the cellar

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.180
to feed the Babadook.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:36.920
"Am I ever gonna see it?"

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:39.100
"One day.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:40.280
When you're bigger."

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:48.960
If the Babadook represents the grief that
Amelia feels over Oskar's death, then Samuel

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.920
will have to eventually reckon with it, too.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.830
He might not have ever known his father enough
to mourn him the way his mom has, but that

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.980
grief is waiting in the wings for when he's
older.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:01.520
This also fits the idea that the Babadook
represents mental illness, as many forms are

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:02.020
hereditary.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:06.560
It's likely that Samuel will one day have
to battle the same monsters, figurative and

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.480
literal, that Amelia's struggled against.

